  "result": "I wrote `tests/unit/intervals.test.ts` with 17 tests and ran `npx vitest run`: **12 pass and 5 fail.** The 5 failures point to a real bug in `mergeIntervals`, not a problem with the tests.\n\n**The bug:** the doc comment says the function should \"merge every pair of blocks that overlaps **or touches**\" and that the result has no blocks that \"touch or overlap.\" But `src/intervals.ts:17` checks `start < last[1]`. That's a strict comparison, so blocks that only share an endpoint are never merged. For example, `[[1, 2], [2, 3]]` comes back as two blocks instead of `[[1, 3]]`.\n\nThese are the 5 tests that fail, all because touching blocks aren't merged:\n- merges intervals that touch at an endpoint\n- merges a chain of touching intervals\n- merges a later-sorted interval touching an earlier one\n- merges a zero-length interval touching another\n- produces sorted output with no touching or overlapping blocks\n\nThese tests pass: empty input, single interval, separate intervals, overlapping intervals, one interval inside another, unsorted input, zero-length intervals, duplicates, negative numbers, input not modified, and new arrays returned rather than the input ones.\n\nAs you asked, I didn't change `src/intervals.ts`. The fix is one character on line 17: change `start < last[1]` to `start <= last[1]`. With that change, all 17 tests should pass. Alternatively, if blocks that only touch are meant to stay separate, the doc comment is what's wrong, and I'd update those 5 tests to match.",
